gm-method: for a generic "clean up this project" ask on a mature repo with a dense existing PRD store (600+ items, 0 pending) and no obvious dead-code signal from a quick codesearch orient pass, the highest-leverage move is dispatching the standing remaining-work workflow (fan-out sweep + adversarial verify + synthesize) rather than inventing an ad-hoc cleanup scope. It surfaced 9 real, individually-verified findings (dead code, a shared-mutable-object correctness bug, a UX raycast inconsistency with a self-contradicting code comment, a silent-failure diagnosability gap, and 3 real unauthenticated-RCE-class security gaps) that a shallow manual pass would have missed. Applying required real live verification per finding (node --check, the project's real test.js suite, live curl against a booted server for the auth gates) rather than trusting the workflow's adversarial-verify pass alone -- the workflow verifies the finding is REAL and WORTH FIXING, not that your eventual fix is correct.